  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 1211 PM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nToday through Saturday... \n\nThe pattern remains relatively stagnant \nover the next 24 hours for central Indiana. In the mid to upper \nlevels, weak theta-e advection will aid in ridge building across the \nMissouri to Mississippi Valleys. This will allow for even warmer \ntemperatures today and tomorrow. Given 850mb temps around 10-11C, \nsurface temps. The record high temperature for Saturday is 73 set in \n1990, of which could be broken as long as skies remain mostly clear \nthrough peak heating. Long nights and clear skies along with a dry \nairmass will continue to lead to large diurnal temperature swings \n(30-35 degrees) especially in more rural areas. \n\nGiven the modest moisture return along with continued subsidence \naloft, there is concern for patchy fog both tonight and Saturday \nnight. Particular areas of concern include stream valleys and well \nirrigated lands.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday night...\n\nA change in the pattern is expected Saturday night as a positively \ntilted shortwave trough moves into the region. An associated surface \nlow over the northern Great Lakes with a cold front extending south \nthrough the Mississippi Valley will aid in advecting warm, moist \nGulf air to Indiana. At the same time, a weak upper level closed low \nshifting northward off of the Carolina coast will aid to reinforce \nthe surface high pressure back into the Ohio Valley. As a result, \nmoisture returns will be limited and confined to a narrow corridor \nalong the cold front. In addition, upper level flow will be \ndisplaced to the north and west of Indiana and is expected to weaken \nwith time as the system passes through Sunday evening. Despite \nguidance suggesting PWATs in the 90th percentile, limited moisture \nand the displacement of synoptic forcing to the north and west \nshould limit precip totals to 0.1-0.25in while restricting any \nprecipitation to a narrow corridor along/just ahead of the front. \nAny chance for embedded thunderstorms carrying isolated higher \nprecipitation rates is limited as forecast profiles at most depict \n100 J/Kg of MUCAPE possibly reaching into south and western portions \nof Indiana. \n\nMonday through Thanksgiving...\n\nThe passage of the cold front Sunday night will usher a return to a \ndry weather pattern with cooler temperatures expected through the \nmiddle of next week. In the wake of the cold front, surface high \npressure will continue to build over the Great Lakes region while \nthe next potential system may start to take shape over the central \nCONUS. Models are suggesting that an upper level trough will start \nto form during the day Tuesday, though how far south the trough digs \nand the trajectory after Tuesday remains an area of discrepancy by \nthe models. The GFS shows the trough digging south into the Gulf \nwith a subsequent surface low quickly becoming closed aloft during \nthe day Thursday. Meanwhile, as the trough digs south, the ECMWF has \nthe southern branch of the trough splitting off and weakening, \nforming a split flow regime with the northern branch of the flow \nimpacting the Great Lakes. Depending on how far south the trough \ndigs, the potential for split flow, and placement/timing of the best \narea of vertical ascent introduce uncertainty in precipitation \nchances for Thanksgiving. Despite the uncertainty, weak \nfrontogenetic forcing from both the GFS and ECMWF suggest \nprecipitation chances towards Thanksgiving remain limited (< 20%) at \nthis current time.",
